NG MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SUNDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...San Juan Mountains\n*WHEN... From 4:30 PM Sunday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS... Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous avalanches large enough to bury a human.\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS... A potent winter storm increases avalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause natural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at www.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\n\n\nBeyer","ends":"2026-11-02T17:00:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Saguache County West of Continental Divide Below 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.fe76f99d50f1dd3da173a38b1d32a5f96ac5311c.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.fe76f99d50f1dd3da173a38b1d32a5f96ac5311c.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued November 1 at 1:50PM MST until November 4 at 5:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall will be possible,\nespecially over the western half of San Luis Valley.\n\n* WHERE...The western half of the San Luis Valley, including\nAntonito, Del Norte, Monte Vista and Alamosa.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 PM MST this afternoon through late Tuesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Runoff from heavy rain and melting snow over the San Juan\nMountains may cause flooding in the San Luis Valley, especially\nMonday and Tuesday as high flows move out of the mountains and\nthrough the Rio Grande and Conejos River.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe Rio Grande, including Del Norte, Monte Vista and Alamosa.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-04